{
  "configuration_id": "config_031",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_005 (RNA-only, scvi_n200, multiprofile, avgpool) achieved 0.650 acc, while config_030 (multimodal, scvi_n200, resnet50, multiprofile, avgpool, concat) dropped to 0.632 acc—adding the image modality with simple concatenation actually hurt performance. This is the most surprising and consequential finding so far. Question: config_031 changes only the fusion strategy from concat to weighted, keeping all other settings identical to config_030. This isolates whether the performance drop is due to the fusion mechanism (concat forcing equal-weight combination of a strong RNA signal with a weak ResNet50 signal) or due to the image features themselves being uninformative/noisy. Why best next experiment: It is a clean single-variable A/B comparison against config_030, directly addressing the key open question of whether the image modality can contribute value under proper integration. If weighted fusion recovers to ≥0.650, it reveals the image carries signal that needs gating; if it stays near 0.632, it strongly indicates ResNet50 features are not useful for this task, redirecting effort toward RNA-only optimization or a stronger image encoder (ViT-L). No other available configuration answers this question with fewer confounds."
}

